WEBVTT

1
00:00:16.199 --> 00:00:20.881
days passed, and the coachman could take me no farther for the sum I had given him.

2
00:00:21.942 --> 00:00:26.965
He set me down at Whitcross, which was not a town but merely a crossroads with a sign

3
00:00:27.045 --> 00:00:28.986
showing the distances to four towns.

4
00:00:30.087 --> 00:00:32.308
The nearest town was ten miles away.

5
00:00:33.448 --> 00:00:38.692
Suddenly I realized that I'd left my parcel, containing my clothing and jewelry, on the coach.

6
00:00:39.833 --> 00:00:43.696
I was absolutely alone and destitute in the midst of the moors.

7
00:00:44.996 --> 00:00:45.917
Evening was coming.

8
00:00:47.078 --> 00:00:48.179
What was I going to do?

9
00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:54.323
I feared that a traveler might pass and wonder why I lingered at the signpost, so I left

10
00:00:54.383 --> 00:00:56.285
the road and waded into the dark heath.

11
00:00:57.525 --> 00:01:00.828
I found shelter for the night under a moss-covered granite crag.

12
00:01:02.012 --> 00:01:05.794
I might have passed a peaceful night, but my heart was broken.

13
00:01:06.995 --> 00:01:11.558
The next morning I returned to Whitcross and from there followed a road until I heard a church

14
00:01:11.598 --> 00:01:18.762
bell. About two o'clock I entered a village, where I immediately noticed a shop with some rolls in

15
00:01:18.782 --> 00:01:19.282
the window.

16
00:01:20.483 --> 00:01:25.386
With a roll, I could regain a degree of energy; without one, it would be difficult to go

17
00:01:25.606 --> 00:01:28.148
on. What did I have to trade?

18
00:01:29.404 --> 00:01:32.785
Only a small silk handkerchief tied at my throat and my gloves.

19
00:01:34.066 --> 00:01:34.946
I entered the shop.

20
00:01:34.966 --> 00:01:36.967
"May I help you?"

21
00:01:37.367 --> 00:01:38.888
inquired the woman at the counter.

22
00:01:40.068 --> 00:01:42.870
I was seized with shame and couldn't make my request.

23
00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:44.650
"I'm tired.

24
00:01:45.711 --> 00:01:46.931
May I sit for a moment?"

25
00:01:48.152 --> 00:01:51.433
Looking disappointed, she pointed to a seat, and I sank into it.

26
00:01:52.634 --> 00:01:55.215
I wanted to weep but restrained myself.

27
00:01:56.455 --> 00:01:58.316
I decided to inquire about employment.

28
00:01:59.404 --> 00:02:01.045
"Are there any dressmakers in the village?"

29
00:02:01.445 --> 00:02:01.965
I asked.

30
00:02:03.486 --> 00:02:04.186
"Two or three.

31
00:02:05.227 --> 00:02:06.067
That's all we need."

32
00:02:06.087 --> 00:02:09.628
"Do you know of anyplace where a servant is wanted?"

33
00:02:09.648 --> 00:02:12.630
"No, I don't think so."

34
00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:18.292
I asked her what most people did here, and she said they either worked on the farms or

35
00:02:18.332 --> 00:02:20.073
at the foundry or in Mr.

36
00:02:20.173 --> 00:02:21.413
Oliver's needle factory.

37
00:02:22.534 --> 00:02:22.954
But Mr.

38
00:02:23.054 --> 00:02:24.415
Oliver didn't employ women.

39
00:02:24.435 --> 00:02:26.715
"What do the women do?"

40
00:02:27.136 --> 00:02:27.656
I asked.

41
00:02:28.964 --> 00:02:30.165
"I have no idea.

42
00:02:31.205 --> 00:02:33.506
Poor people must get by as they can."

43
00:02:33.526 --> 00:02:36.528
A customer came in, and I left.

44
00:02:37.728 --> 00:02:40.189
For most of the afternoon I walked through the village.

45
00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:46.232
I approached houses and then wandered away, realizing I had no right to ask for help.

46
00:02:47.432 --> 00:02:48.873
But then I remembered the church.

47
00:02:50.134 --> 00:02:55.296
People who arrive at a place without friends sometimes apply to the minister for introductions.

48
00:02:56.592 --> 00:02:59.113
At the parsonage an old woman answered the door.

49
00:02:59.133 --> 00:03:02.034
"Is the minister in?"

50
00:03:02.434 --> 00:03:02.954
I asked.

51
00:03:02.974 --> 00:03:07.515
"No, his father died suddenly.

52
00:03:08.736 --> 00:03:13.537
He's three miles away at Moor House and will likely stay two more weeks."

53
00:03:13.557 --> 00:03:16.118
"Is there a lady of the house?"

54
00:03:17.338 --> 00:03:22.900
The woman shook her head and said that the minister wasn't married and she was only a servant.

55
00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:28.763
I was not yet ready to beg so I stumbled away, but I kept thinking of those rolls

56
00:03:28.803 --> 00:03:29.744
in the village shop.

57
00:03:30.945 --> 00:03:34.567
I found the shop again, and removing my handkerchief, I went in.

58
00:03:34.587 --> 00:03:37.669
"Would you give me a roll for this handkerchief?"

59
00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:40.531
The woman looked at me with suspicion.

60
00:03:40.551 --> 00:03:44.254
"No, I never sell things that way."

61
00:03:45.455 --> 00:03:49.037
Almost desperate, I asked for half a roll, and she again refused.

62
00:03:49.057 --> 00:03:52.199
"How do I know where you got that handkerchief?"

63
00:03:52.619 --> 00:03:53.260
she demanded.

64
00:03:55.296 --> 00:03:56.377
"Would you take my gloves?"

65
00:03:56.397 --> 00:03:58.998
"No!" she cried.

66
00:03:59.018 --> 00:04:01.439
"What would I do with those?"

67
00:04:02.599 --> 00:04:05.461
Reader, it's not pleasant to dwell on these details.

68
00:04:06.681 --> 00:04:10.683
Let me just say that I begged some bread from a farmer that evening and spent the night

69
00:04:10.723 --> 00:04:11.203
in the woods.

70
00:04:12.404 --> 00:04:15.585
Toward morning it rained, and the whole next day was wet.

71
00:04:16.766 --> 00:04:20.867
I ate only once, when I begged a girl for the cold porridge she was about to throw

72
00:04:20.907 --> 00:04:21.748
into a pig trough.

73
00:04:23.004 --> 00:04:25.245
I sought work all day but found nothing.

74
00:04:26.366 --> 00:04:31.769
By nightfall I had strayed far from the village, but in the blackness, a small point of light

75
00:04:31.849 --> 00:04:32.350
sprang up.

76
00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:36.312
Something about the light pulled me toward it through the rain.

77
00:04:37.613 --> 00:04:40.714
After crossing a marsh, I came to a long, low house.

78
00:04:41.975 --> 00:04:46.978
I peered through a window and saw the candle that had been my beacon burning on a table.

79
00:04:48.399 --> 00:04:54.783
An elderly woman was knitting, and two young women, dressed in mourning clothes, sat near the hearth.

80
00:04:56.164 --> 00:04:59.826
The girls appeared to be consulting a dictionary while they read aloud.

81
00:05:01.067 --> 00:05:06.511
As I watched them, I learned that their names were Mary and Diana and that they were studying

82
00:05:06.571 --> 00:05:13.575
German. The housekeeper was named Hannah, and they were all waiting for the return of the girls' brother,

83
00:05:15.508 --> 00:05:15.728
St. John.

84
00:05:15.748 --> 00:05:21.391
I was so absorbed in their conversation that I forgot my own wants until Hannah began to prepare

85
00:05:21.431 --> 00:05:25.473
dinner. I knocked on the door, and Hannah opened it.

86
00:05:25.493 --> 00:05:27.535
"What do you want?"

87
00:05:27.995 --> 00:05:29.996
She surveyed me by the light of a candle.

88
00:05:30.016 --> 00:05:34.398
"A night's shelter in a shed or barn and a morsel of bread."

89
00:05:35.599 --> 00:05:39.801
Distrust, the very feeling I dreaded, appeared on Hannah's face.

90
00:05:41.162 --> 00:05:44.404
"I'll give you some bread, but we can't take in a vagrant."

91
00:05:45.868 --> 00:05:47.869
"But where shall I go if you drive me away?

92
00:05:49.130 --> 00:05:50.831
Oh, please let me speak to your mistresses!"

93
00:05:51.271 --> 00:05:52.052
I implored her.

94
00:05:52.072 --> 00:05:59.736
"I don't know what you're doing here, but if anyone's with you—burglars and the like—tell them we're not

95
00:05:59.816 --> 00:06:02.857
alone. We have a gentleman and dogs and guns."

96
00:06:04.198 --> 00:06:05.859
She slammed the door and bolted it.

97
00:06:05.879 --> 00:06:10.061
"But I'll die if you turn me away," I said weakly.

98
00:06:10.081 --> 00:06:15.861
"Everyone must die, but this is not your time," said a nearby voice.

99
00:06:15.881 --> 00:06:17.962
"Who's there?"

100
00:06:18.383 --> 00:06:19.423
I cried in terror.

101
00:06:20.684 --> 00:06:22.626
A man went up to the door and knocked.

102
00:06:23.806 --> 00:06:24.927
Hannah opened the door.

103
00:06:24.947 --> 00:06:26.909
"Oh, Mr.

104
00:06:26.969 --> 00:06:27.549
St. John!

105
00:06:28.670 --> 00:06:32.392
Your sisters have been so worried, and there are bad folks about.

106
00:06:33.593 --> 00:06:35.895
A beggar woman—why there she is!

107
00:06:35.955 --> 00:06:36.695
Move along!"

108
00:06:36.715 --> 00:06:39.357
"Hush, Hannah," he said.

109
00:06:39.377 --> 00:06:42.920
"You've done your duty in excluding her.

110
00:06:44.116 --> 00:06:45.997
Now let me do mine in admitting her."

111
00:06:47.218 --> 00:06:50.279
Soon I stood before the hearth that I had seen through the window.

112
00:06:51.540 --> 00:06:54.942
St. John bade me sit down while his sisters fetched bread and milk.

113
00:06:54.962 --> 00:06:59.104
"Try to eat," Diana said as she gently fed me.

114
00:07:00.325 --> 00:07:02.026
Mary removed my wet bonnet.

115
00:07:02.046 --> 00:07:03.967
"What's your name?"

116
00:07:03.987 --> 00:07:06.208
"Jane Elliott."

117
00:07:07.269 --> 00:07:10.530
I had earlier decided to assume an alias to avoid detection.

118
00:07:10.550 --> 00:07:13.332
"What account can you give of yourself?"

119
00:07:13.796 --> 00:07:14.236
said St.

120
00:07:14.276 --> 00:07:18.619
John. "Please, sir, spare me from conversation tonight.

121
00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:22.822
My breath is short, and I feel a spasm when I speak."

122
00:07:24.082 --> 00:07:25.824
All three silently surveyed me.

123
00:07:25.844 --> 00:07:29.546
"Let's go into the parlor and talk this over," St.

124
00:07:29.566 --> 00:07:30.887
John said to his sisters.

125
00:07:32.207 --> 00:07:38.932
A few minutes later Diana returned with instructions for Hannah, who escorted me upstairs to a warm, dry

126
00:07:39.032 --> 00:07:39.252
bed.
